      ":question/id": "6e6c66f6-6133-59f6-a79d-0cbf1615accf",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-113665",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We apply the power rule with $n=20$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{20}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{20+1}}{20+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{21}}{21})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{21}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{21})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{21}\\cdot 21{x}^{20} & = \\\\ {x}^{20}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186233175,
      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/easy"
      },
      ":question/id": "7949e7bf-09d1-5ce9-ac3c-0d7f26b2e6c4",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-113670",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{17}$ and ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=17$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{17}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{17+1}}{17+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{18}}{18})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{18}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{18})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{18}\\cdot 18\\,{x}^{17} & = \\\\ {x}^{17}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186234186,
      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/easy"
      },
      ":question/id": "48cc16d0-0280-564d-bcf3-e481ad8d20ab",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-66126",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "If we write the integrand as a power of $x$, we have ${x}^{0}=1.$ Therefore, we can use the power rule with $n=0$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ 1+0 & = \\\\ 1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
    {
      ":db/id": 17592186234300,
      ":question/difficulty": {
        ":db/ident": ":question.difficulty/easy"
