      ":question/id": "a4e1eabf-8b25-5ba2-8660-9b7cb6a5610c",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-29994",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $F(x)$ given that $f(x)={x}^{4}$ and ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply the power rule with $n=4$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4+1}}{4+1}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C) & = \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{5})+0 & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5{x}^{4} & = \\\\ {x}^{4}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    }
